    "Based on ridership data, the busiest Amtrak route that does not stop at either Lorton station (Lorton or Lorton-adjacent stops on the Northeast Corridor/Virginia lines) is the **Northeast Regional** or the **Acela** service between **New York City (Penn Station) and Boston (South Station)**.\n\nWhile the Northeast Regional and Acela serve the entire Northeast Corridor (including the Virginia lines that pass through Lorton), the specific segment between New York and Boston operates independently of the Virginia-bound trains and handles the highest volume of passengers in the Amtrak system.",
    "Based on available ridership data, the busiest Amtrak route that does not stop at either Lorton Station (which serves the Northeast Corridor/Long Distance routes in Virginia) is the **Northeast Regional** (or the **Acela**) operating on the segment between **New York City (Penn Station) and Boston (South Station)**.\n\nWhile the Northeast Regional and Acela are the busiest overall services, many of their trains extend south to Virginia and stop at Lorton. However, because the corridor between New York and Boston is so densely traveled and operates as a distinct high-frequency segment, the ridership on the \"North End\" of the corridor far exceeds any other non-Lorton route.\n\nIf you are looking for a completely separate named route (one that does not share a line with the Virginia-bound trains), the **Pacific Surfliner** (serving San Luis Obispo, Santa Barbara, Los Angeles, and San Diego) is typically the busiest route in the Western U.S. and has no connection to the Lorton stations.",
    "Based on available ridership data, the busiest Amtrak route that does not stop at either Lorton Station (which serves the Northeast Corridor/Long Distance lines in Virginia) is the **Northeast Regional** service between **New York City (Penn Station) and Boston (South Station)**.\n\nWhile the Northeast Regional as a whole is one of Amtrak's most used services, it operates as a network of overlapping segments. The high-volume corridor between New York and Boston operates independently of the segments that travel south toward Virginia and Lorton.\n\nIf you are looking for a specific named long-distance route (rather than a corridor segment), the **California Zephyr** or the **South West Chief** are among the busiest routes that have no connection to the Lorton stations."
